# Service Patterns (`@stridetime/core`)

## Service Class Structure

```typescript
import {
  taskRepo as defaultTaskRepo,
  type TaskRepository,
  type StrideDatabase,
} from '@stridetime/db';
import type { Task, CreateTaskInput } from '@stridetime/types';
import { ValidationError, NotFoundError } from '@stridetime/types';
import { sanitizeFields } from '../utils/sanitize';

export class TaskService {
  constructor(
    private taskRepo: TaskRepository = defaultTaskRepo,
  ) {}

  async create(db: StrideDatabase, params: CreateTaskInput): Promise<Task> {
    // 1. Sanitize inputs
    const sanitized = sanitizeFields(params);
    // 2. Validate synchronously
    this.validateCreate(sanitized);
    // 3. Async existence checks
    const project = await this.taskRepo.findById(db, sanitized.projectId);
    if (!project) throw new NotFoundError('Project not found');
    // 4. Business logic
    // 5. Repo call
    return this.taskRepo.create(db, { ...sanitized, userId: params.userId });
  }

  async update(db: StrideDatabase, id: string, params: UpdateTaskInput): Promise<Task> {
    const sanitized = sanitizeFields(params);
    this.validateUpdate(sanitized);
    const existing = await this.taskRepo.findById(db, id);
    if (!existing) throw new NotFoundError('Task not found');
    await this.taskRepo.update(db, id, sanitized);
    return { ...existing, ...sanitized };
  }

  async delete(db: StrideDatabase, id: string): Promise<void> {
    const existing = await this.taskRepo.findById(db, id);
    if (!existing) throw new NotFoundError('Task not found');
    await this.taskRepo.delete(db, id);
  }

  private validateCreate(params: CreateTaskInput): void {
    if (!params.title?.trim()) {
      throw new ValidationError('title', 'Task title is required');
    }
  }

  private validateUpdate(params: UpdateTaskInput): void {
    if (params.title !== undefined && !params.title?.trim()) {
      throw new ValidationError('title', 'Task title cannot be empty');
    }
  }
}

// Always export both class and singleton
export const taskService = new TaskService();
```

## Method Execution Order

Every `create` / `update` method must follow this order — no exceptions:

1. `sanitizeFields(params)` — trim strings, normalize values
2. `this.validateXxx(sanitized)` — synchronous validation, throws `ValidationError`
3. Async DB existence checks — throws `NotFoundError` if missing
4. Business logic (calculations, transformations)
5. Repo call(s)
6. Return result

## Validation Rules

- Validation methods are `private` and named `validateCreate`, `validateUpdate`, etc.
- They throw `ValidationError(field, message)` — never return booleans
- Synchronous validation runs before any async work
- Required fields, format checks, and business rule violations all live here

## DI Pattern

- Constructor takes typed repo interfaces with default singleton values
- This enables test injection without a DI container
- Never call `new TaskRepository()` inside a service method — use the injected instance

## sanitizeFields

Every `create` and `update` method must call `sanitizeFields` before anything else. This trims string inputs and normalizes falsy values. Do not skip it even if inputs seem clean.
